---
// "A new Terraform, reimagined for AI." — the best-of-both grid (Eron's headline, 2026-11-07; it
// replaced "Built to run the whole stack", the fan-out figure and the encryption HCL). Seven tiles,
// each wearing a provenance chip row, Terraform · OpenTofu · Turf, so the band reads as a scorecard
// the way /compatibility/ does in depth; the console is a double-wide tile with its still, the band's
// one visual. Sources: /compatibility/'s strengths table and matrix notes, the 1.2–1.4 release notes,
// and the Restate sentence in Restate's own words (D18, revised 2026-10-18). Rules kept: "HCP" never
// appears (say "HashiCorp's hosted platform"); no benchmark figure; `coming soon` at the capability
// level; the marks' symbols are the hero's, so none here; the headline uses "Terraform" as the
// category Turf re-does, never as Turf's name (CLAUDE.md, "Trademark").
import Still from './Still.astro';
---

<section class="band alt" id="features">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="band-head">
      <h2>A new Terraform, reimagined for AI.</h2>
      <p>The best of Terraform and OpenTofu, run by one distributed engine that an agent can drive —
        and the things neither has: an apply that can ask, and a console that shows every round.</p>
      <p class="provenance prov-legend">Where each one comes from:
        <span class="chip tf"><span class="sw"></span>Terraform</span>
        <span class="chip otf"><span class="sw"></span>OpenTofu</span>
        <span class="chip turf"><span class="sw"></span>Turf</span></p>
    </div>

    <div class="solutions grid4">
      <div class="sol">
        <span class="prov"><span class="chip tf"><span class="sw"></span>Terraform</span><span class="chip otf"><span class="sw"></span>OpenTofu</span></span>
        <h3>Modules, as they are.</h3>
        <p>Registry, git and local sources; your private registries and mirrors; provider-defined
          functions. <code>init</code> installs and locks them, as always. No new language to learn.</p>
        <a class="runit" href="/compatibility/">every construct, scored →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sol">
        <span class="prov"><span class="chip otf"><span class="sw"></span>OpenTofu</span></span>
        <h3>State encryption, enforced.</h3>
        <p>OpenTofu’s <code>encryption</code> block, with a passphrase or a key provider. Migrating an
          existing state is a no-op run; <code>enforced = true</code> refuses plaintext.</p>
        <a class="runit" href="/docs/environment/state-encryption/">state encryption →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sol">
        <span class="prov"><span class="chip tf"><span class="sw"></span>Terraform</span></span>
        <h3>Terraform actions, by overlay.</h3>
        <p>The 1.16 action model — six lifecycle events, <code>on_failure</code>, a plan-time condition —
          so an Ansible playbook, a certification run or a smoke test is a step in the plan.
          <code>*.turf.hcl</code> overlays add triggers to modules you don’t own.</p>
        <a class="runit" href="/docs/authoring/actions-and-overlays/">actions and overlays →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sol">
        <span class="prov"><span class="chip tf"><span class="sw"></span>Terraform</span><span class="chip turf"><span class="sw"></span>Turf</span></span>
        <h3>Stacks, in rounds.</h3>
        <p>A cluster, then the chart that runs on it: one <code>up</code> plans what it can know, defers
          the rest and replans until converged. What Terraform Stacks does on HashiCorp’s hosted
          platform, with plain modules, on your own engine.</p>
        <a class="runit" href="/docs/concepts/rounds/">rounds and deferrals →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sol">
        <span class="prov"><span class="chip turf"><span class="sw"></span>Turf</span></span>
        <h3>Turf actions: an apply that asks.</h3>
        <p>A step of the apply that pauses for a check, a notification or a question — to an agent, or
          to you — then continues under the same approved plan. Neither Terraform nor OpenTofu has one.</p>
        <a class="runit" href="/docs/concepts/turf-actions/">turf actions →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sol">
        <span class="prov"><span class="chip turf"><span class="sw"></span>Turf</span></span>
        <h3>Clusterable.</h3>
        <p>Run the engine as replicas behind one restate-server. Refresh, plan and apply fan out per
          resource; a run outlives the pod it started on; the cloud API, not the tool, is the ceiling.
          <span class="pill soon">coming soon</span> per-provider flow control.</p>
        <a class="runit" href="/docs/cluster/topology/">Turf in a cluster →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sol wide">
        <div class="sol-text">
          <span class="prov"><span class="chip turf"><span class="sw"></span>Turf</span></span>
          <h3>A console.</h3>
          <p>Every workspace’s runs, rounds and approvals — who approved which plan, by digest — with a
            link in every result. Read-only; on your laptop or in the cluster.</p>
          <a class="runit" href="/docs/concepts/engine-and-console/">the engine and the console →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="sol-still">
          <Still id="console" src="/console/aicr-converged.png"
            alt="The Turf console: a run in two rounds, converged, with a lane for every resource of the stack."
            caption="The Turf console: every run, round and approval, visible in one place. Click to enlarge." />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="also"><b>Also:</b> planfiles you plan here and apply there, a permission tier per server,
      OpenTelemetry built in, ephemeral module outputs —
      <a href="/compatibility/">scored honestly against Terraform 1.16 and OpenTofu 1.13 →</a></p>
    <p class="also">The engine runs on <a href="https://restate.dev">Restate</a>, in Restate’s words “a lightweight
      runtime to turn AI agents, workflows, and backend services into durable processes.”
      <a href="/engine/#engine">How the engine is built →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
